{"title":"2022 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 15","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Diagram\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Diagram</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_1_(Inscribed_Angle_Theorem)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1 (Inscribed Angle Theorem)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_2_(Brahmagupta‘s_Formula_and_Pythagorean_Theorem)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2 (Brahmagupta‘s Formula and Pythagorean Theorem)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_3_(Circumradius&#39;s_Formula)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3 (Circumradius's Formula)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_4_(Complete_Bash_Using_Brahmagupta’s_and_Parameshvara’s_Formulas)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4 (Complete Bash Using Brahmagupta’s and Parameshvara’s Formulas)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Remark\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Remark</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-10\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_TheBeautyofMath_(Simple_Pythagorean_Triples)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">10</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by TheBeautyofMath (Simple Pythagorean Triples)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-11\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">11</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_15&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Quadrilateral <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/e/f9efaf9474c5658c4089523e2aff4e11488f8603.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABCD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"60\" height=\"14\" /> with side lengths <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/5/6f535e5972de04fc798ed71547b3ec2443a6f2c4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB=7, BC=24, CD=20, DA=15$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"303\" height=\"18\" /> is inscribed in a circle. The area interior to the circle but exterior to the quadrilateral can be written in the form <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/3/4/7344020e44515eec98dd40cbd92358c00e57f7f6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{a\\pi-b}{c},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"62\" height=\"39\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/b/f3b28547efd22be1c4a05fa193cd7038fc65d71b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a,b,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"29\" height=\"16\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/7/3372c1cb6d68cf97c2d231acc0b47b95a9ed04cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" /> are positive integers such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/7/3372c1cb6d68cf97c2d231acc0b47b95a9ed04cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" /> have no common prime factor. What is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/3/24323ad615514d3902fb6720a462f134c52cf8f2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a+b+c?$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"80\" height=\"16\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/0/3/d0378a8f65f70bf7be0b3cad030cafb4835197ba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A) } 260 \\qquad \\textbf{(B) } 855 \\qquad \\textbf{(C) } 1235 \\qquad \\textbf{(D) } 1565 \\qquad \\textbf{(E) } 1997$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"473\" height=\"18\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Diagram\">Diagram</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_15&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Diagram\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/4/6f4d2e5edead8a1a9eddcc202b507b2a4f6e8023.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] /* Made by MRENTHUSIASM */ size(200); pair O, A, B, C, D; O = origin; A = (-25/2,0); C = (25/2,0); B = intersectionpoints(Circle(A,7),Circle(C,24))[0]; D = intersectionpoints(Circle(A,15),Circle(C,20))[1]; fill(Circle(O,25/2),yellow); fill(A--B--C--D--cycle,white); dot(&quot;$A$&quot;,A,1.5*W,linewidth(4)); dot(&quot;$B$&quot;,B,1.5*dir(B),linewidth(4)); dot(&quot;$C$&quot;,C,1.5*E,linewidth(4)); dot(&quot;$D$&quot;,D,1.5*dir(D),linewidth(4)); dot(O,linewidth(4)); draw(Circle(O,25/2)); draw(A--B--C--D--cycle); label(&quot;$7$&quot;,midpoint(A--B),rotate(90)*dir(midpoint(A--B)--A)); label(&quot;$24$&quot;,midpoint(B--C),rotate(-90)*dir(midpoint(B--C)--B)); label(&quot;$20$&quot;,midpoint(C--D),rotate(-90)*dir(midpoint(C--D)--C)); label(&quot;$15$&quot;,midpoint(D--A),rotate(90)*dir(midpoint(D--A)--D)); [/asy]\" width=\"335\" height=\"302\" />\n~MRENTHUSIASM\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_1_.28Inscribed_Angle_Theorem.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1_(Inscribed_Angle_Theorem)\">Solution 1 (Inscribed Angle Theorem)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_15&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1 (Inscribed Angle Theorem)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Opposite angles of every cyclic quadrilateral are supplementary, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/b/10b835ac4f345b1691b1f847eb0bd5445af3efd0.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\angle B + \\angle D = 180^{\\circ}.\\]\" width=\"140\" height=\"14\" /> Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/5/f552ad3b30bd7c5ebb14bf5d724705a8e4881e55.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7^2+24^2=15^2+20^2=25^2,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"219\" height=\"20\" /> we claim that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/4/814511667729f0034a9ec9be0df29364833911b1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC=25,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"77\" height=\"18\" /> and we can prove it by contradiction:\n</p>\n<ul><li>If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/8/4/a84693a2558139000bc92e3ec15f860bc32c9584.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC&lt;25,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"77\" height=\"18\" /> then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/1/2918f7d23f9ee8c04baabad86100d68023ec03f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/e/1/ae15cd6fc137587ad1676133c8342dc210dc6364.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> are both acute angles, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/4/1/641333311374b30d419d1d5f76dc6750c97b13e9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle B + \\angle D \\neq 180^{\\circ}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"144\" height=\"18\" /></li></ul>\n<ul><li>If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/6/3/86357234edcbf4ed9615e80dae84ca3e431ed97f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC&gt;25,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"77\" height=\"18\" /> then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/1/2918f7d23f9ee8c04baabad86100d68023ec03f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/e/1/ae15cd6fc137587ad1676133c8342dc210dc6364.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> are both obtuse angles, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/4/1/641333311374b30d419d1d5f76dc6750c97b13e9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle B + \\angle D \\neq 180^{\\circ}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"144\" height=\"18\" /></li></ul>\n<p>Therefore, we conclude that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/4/814511667729f0034a9ec9be0df29364833911b1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC=25,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"77\" height=\"18\" /> from which <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/5/bb5ffca17e8f5d2f04ce60175add21df4eef9a47.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle B = \\angle D = 90^{\\circ}$\" width=\"128\" height=\"13\" /> by the converse of the Pythagorean theorem.\n</p><p>By the inscribed angle theorem, it follows that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/5/12517bbc31cb9026c71299b7a0d232d07e60f284.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{AC}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"18\" /> is the diameter of the circle. So, the radius of the circle is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/e/d/7edac6b8b1212965c20ae9070eb0d85e231ebe13.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r=\\frac{AC}{2}=\\frac{25}{2}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"118\" height=\"39\" />\n</p><p>The area of the requested region is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/a/b/eabc501f3d3709f5610b40fa8eef1209001f1b06.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\pi r^2 - \\frac12\\cdot AB\\cdot BC - \\frac12\\cdot AD\\cdot DC = \\frac{625\\pi}{4}-\\frac{168}{2}-\\frac{300}{2}=\\frac{625\\pi-936}{4}.\\]\" width=\"495\" height=\"38\" />\nTherefore, the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/8/bc86ec89d7848514bf992fefd233459f2596840d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a+b+c=\\boxed{\\textbf{(D) } 1565}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"184\" height=\"30\" />\n</p><p>~MRENTHUSIASM\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_2_.28Brahmagupta.E2.80.98s_Formula_and_Pythagorean_Theorem.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2_(Brahmagupta‘s_Formula_and_Pythagorean_Theorem)\">Solution 2 (Brahmagupta‘s Formula and Pythagorean Theorem)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_15&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2 (Brahmagupta‘s Formula and Pythagorean Theorem)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>When we look at the side lengths of the quadrilateral we see <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/a/e0a0db32027a732ac57d37ef2ae9bb150f65b108.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/a/19a706df7e216ab0761ae29b30c03da166cd7768.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$24,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"22\" height=\"15\" /> which screams out <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/6/6/66644fd9f411299c3c1eabf151b5f601c9ef6c21.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$25$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> because of Pythagorean triplets. As a result, we can draw a line through points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> to make a diameter of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/7/b/67bc7bd42e6cb90851b324c8f2955a8d3021b6cb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$25.$\" width=\"21\" height=\"12\" /> See Solution 1 for a rigorous proof.\n</p><p>This can also be shown using the Law of Cosines: Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/6/d/46d025f673dbd64492b128c8cc3effed81600c74.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7^2+24^2-2\\cdot7\\cdot24\\cdot\\cos B=15^2+20^2-2\\cdot15\\cdot20\\cdot\\cos D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"454\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/2/e/92e670d5fd4c785ed03e08b1a1f427f35490aa27.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\cos B + \\cos D = 0,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"147\" height=\"18\" /> it follows that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/4/254a4ae4e1de13096652ed75c7e3ef2db235e109.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\cos B = \\cos D = 0.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"149\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>Since the diameter is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/8/b/08bb14a160397cb2d3fc9ef2f8e6be49951957a1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$25,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"22\" height=\"16\" /> we can see the area of the circle is just <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/4/9/c497825ab961cc058cf4cc285ebc3a1198419d32.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{625\\pi}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"44\" height=\"38\" /> from the formula of the area of the circle with just a diameter. \n</p><p>Then we can use Brahmagupta Formula <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/b/8/0b86943036292c00b990762ddcc75ce9c07fcc04.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{(s - a)(s - b)(s - c)(s - d)}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"234\" height=\"35\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/6/bb60e0857a2c0a2c47acfbe81781032feb1f7150.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a,b,c,d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> are side lengths, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/7/f37bba504894945c07a32f5496d74299a37aa51c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" /> is semi-perimeter to find the area of the quadrilateral. \n</p><p>You can find the answer to this quickly by noting that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/5/f25199e89e79cbdece418fe351c43a706535a889.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{(33-7)(33-24)(33-20)(33-15)} = \\sqrt{(26)(9)(13)(18)} = \\sqrt{2^2 \\cdot 3^4 \\cdot 13^2} = 2 \\cdot 9 \\cdot 13 = 234$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"726\" height=\"35\" />. \n</p><p>Another way to find the area of the quadrilateral is to take it apart into two right triangles.\n</p><p>Then, the quadrilateral's area is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/1/1012593aea8ec59f0ba8962b381e7d523db7394f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{24\\cdot7}{2}+\\frac{20\\cdot15}{2}=84+150=234.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"270\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p>So now the area of the region we are trying to find is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/7/1/97120c5d94b6a07d99bb2627c9bf7f7875e69382.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{625\\pi}{4} - 234 = \\frac{625\\pi-936}{4}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"215\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p>Therefore, the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/8/bc86ec89d7848514bf992fefd233459f2596840d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a+b+c=\\boxed{\\textbf{(D) } 1565}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"184\" height=\"30\" />\n</p><p>~Gdking ~Oinava ~MemChess225 ~<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php/User:South\">South</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_3_.28Circumradius.27s_Formula.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3_(Circumradius's_Formula)\">Solution 3 (Circumradius's Formula)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_15&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3 (Circumradius&#39;s Formula)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We can guess that this quadrilateral is actually made of two right triangles: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/b/9/eb94e3d043aa1652f6933df7dfc4a760ae51442c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle CDA$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> has a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/8/0a8eee2858682e78873404596d26bcbc3c216470.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3 \\text{-} 4 \\text{-} 5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"40\" height=\"14\" /> ratio in the side lengths, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> is a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/0/a/50a6119f23ad44b1ba72b5911edf5ec7845103c8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7 \\text{-} 24 \\text{-} 25$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"58\" height=\"14\" /> triangle. \n(See Solution 1 for a proof.)\n</p><p>Next, we can choose one of these triangles and use the circumradius formula to find the radius. Let's choose the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/d/c/3dc6f0b7144e8af28215163ee6a151a47e21bb00.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$15-20-25$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"101\" height=\"15\" /> triangle. The area of the triangle is equal to the product of the side lengths divided by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> times the circumradius. Therefore, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/6/4e6ee269828bf4d4c6bf084a8e472debf83f1a98.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$150 = \\frac{15\\cdot20\\cdot25}{4r}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"138\" height=\"38\" />. Solving this simple algebraic equation gives us <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/b/f9b0ce49905a05adaea1aa0cdcb0f5632188110e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r = \\frac{25}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"53\" height=\"37\" />. \n</p><p>Plugging in the values, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/9/bf90b2cdf25538459e04e73133bf50abda0c7d49.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{25}{2}^2\\cdot\\pi - \\left(\\frac{15\\cdot20}{2}+\\frac{7\\cdot24}{2}\\right) = \\frac{625\\cdot\\pi}{4} - 234$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"353\" height=\"46\" />. Rewriting this gives us <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/8/3/0831bc6d9990d97270caf0e3b1a9d62bd54610e1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{625\\pi-936}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"94\" height=\"38\" />.\n</p><p>Therefore, adding these values gets us <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/f/4cfd187031ffa78c7a129bc30dfe1c70e2933751.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(D) } 1565}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"90\" height=\"30\" />\n</p><p>~<a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=OrenSH&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"OrenSH (page does not exist)\">orenbad</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_4_.28Complete_Bash_Using_Brahmagupta.E2.80.99s_and_Parameshvara.E2.80.99s_Formulas.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4_(Complete_Bash_Using_Brahmagupta’s_and_Parameshvara’s_Formulas)\">Solution 4 (Complete Bash Using Brahmagupta’s and Parameshvara’s Formulas)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_15&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4 (Complete Bash Using Brahmagupta’s and Parameshvara’s Formulas)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>To start, we can apply a couple formulas to find the circumradius and the area of the cyclic quadrilateral, and then just subtract the area of the quadrilateral from the area of the circle, and that is our final answer. This formula is really tedious with many calculations so don't do this in a contest. To find the circumradius we have: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/3/1a3cab6b3a509ae62403087e9d20a24945fbab1d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{Circumradius} = \\frac{1}{4} \\sqrt{\\frac{(ab + cd)(ac + bd)(ad + bc)}{(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)(s-d)}}\\]\" width=\"371\" height=\"53\" />\nIf we let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/5/0256eebf93080573d929f5c6e942534309eaae21.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a = 7, b = 24, c = 20, \\text{and}, d = 15$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"256\" height=\"18\" />, that means that the semi-perimeter is just <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/4/8/648b4c31a153e5bea103dda68acde06c9515e780.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\dfrac{7+24+20+15}{2} = 33\\]\" width=\"174\" height=\"38\" />. Note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/7/f37bba504894945c07a32f5496d74299a37aa51c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" /> stands for the semi-perimeter. The calculations will take very long so Im just going to skip to the answer of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/1/4/114235fc11e619c19fc04c5e2ad1162583b929c2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\dfrac{25}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"24\" height=\"38\" /> as our circumradius. This means that the area of the circle is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/8/308e49f2171da6e8fb430aff1ed8805873896ebd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\dfrac{625}{4}\\pi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"44\" height=\"38\" />. Now applying Brahmagupta's formula to find the area of a cyclic quadrilateral which is just: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/9/19969870563da33cddd5c7dc77ac056dfda620d8.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\sqrt{(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)(s-d)}\\]\" width=\"230\" height=\"32\" />. Again we will use the same representations as these letters from before, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/5/0256eebf93080573d929f5c6e942534309eaae21.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a = 7, b = 24, c = 20, \\text{and}, d = 15$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"256\" height=\"18\" />. Plugging in the numbers we have that the area is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/5/815c3c71386b6a6a5d2b338754331461641cc8d7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$234$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"27\" height=\"13\" />. To express our answer in the form that was asked at the top we have: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/d/e2d066018c6d81ff6f5937b338f314068a7b4321.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\dfrac{625\\pi-936}{4}\\]\" width=\"88\" height=\"38\" />, so our final answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/a/b/eabe6a7a09cb99c89a5cd8611c7a70b7f1d54312.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[625+936+4 = \\boxed{\\textbf{(D) } 1565}.\\]\" width=\"219\" height=\"28\" />\n</p><p>-jb2015007\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Remark\">Remark</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_15&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Remark\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>If short on time we can observe that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/f/8/df8ce93516dc68f34fba7e4dc307bc76e5172f8d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7{-}24{-}25$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"76\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/0/f/80f0a767575f17a3f6f76aacb99a4274d402f9d9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$15{-}20{-}25$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"85\" height=\"15\" /> are both very common pythagorean triples, so we can guess that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/e/e/8eee81245b3e2b36321fb11f98fa7f4ede786028.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC=25.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"77\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>Remark by ~AMCY0288\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_1\">Video Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_15&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/ZHuInvG82PY\">https://youtu.be/ZHuInvG82PY</a> \n</p><p>~Education, the Study of Everything\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_2\">Video Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_15&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/Ov9AA7veKKk\">https://youtu.be/Ov9AA7veKKk</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_by_TheBeautyofMath_.28Simple_Pythagorean_Triples.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_TheBeautyofMath_(Simple_Pythagorean_Triples)\">Video Solution by TheBeautyofMath (Simple Pythagorean Triples)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_15&amp;action=edit&amp;section=10\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by TheBeautyofMath (Simple Pythagorean Triples)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/0kkc4-y8TkU?t=1632\">https://youtu.be/0kkc4-y8TkU?t=1632</a>\n</p><p>~IceMatrix\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_15&amp;action=edit&amp;section=11\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_AMC_10A\" title=\"2022 AMC 10A\">2022 AMC 10A</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_AMC_10A_Problems\" title=\"2022 AMC 10A Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_AMC_10A_Answer_Key\" title=\"2022 AMC 10A Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c13\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_14\" title=\"2022 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 14\">Problem 14</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_16\" title=\"2022 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 16\">Problem 16</